---
title: Use the CLI
description: Install the optional terminal launcher, preview a C++ project, and run its build, test, and application tasks.
section: How-to guides
order: 1
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use the CLI to create projects and run tasks from a terminal. It uses the same project model as the Neovim plugin. The example below creates a C++20 application with conventional headers.

## Install the launcher

Download and extract a source archive from [GitHub releases](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/releases), or [use a checkout](/docs/start-here/#local-development). Keep that folder intact: `bin/rivet` loads Lua modules from it.

Add its `bin` directory to your shell's `PATH`, replacing the example with the actual path:

```sh
export PATH="/absolute/path/to/Rivet/bin:$PATH"
```

Add the same line to your shell configuration if you want it in future terminals.

## Check the tools

The launcher needs Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT. Run:

```sh
rivet doctor
```

It uses `lua` by default. To use LuaJIT for this and subsequent commands in the terminal, set:

```sh
export RIVET_LUA=luajit
```

The C++ example needs CMake 3.30+, Ninja, and a compiler accepting C++20. Doctor reports available tools; the build below verifies that they work together. See [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) for module-capable toolchains and other languages.

## Create a first project

From the directory that should contain the new `engine` folder, preview the project:

```sh
rivet new ./engine \
  --standard 20 --modules false --std-modules false \
  --layout feature --style LLVM --preview
```

Read the file plan, then create it with the same options:

```sh
rivet new ./engine \
  --standard 20 --modules false --std-modules false \
  --layout feature --style LLVM
```

To select a particular compiler, add `--compiler /path/to/clang++` to both commands. Rivet records that local path in ignored `CMakeUserPresets.json`.

## Build, test, and run

Run each command after the previous one finishes:

```sh
rivet build --root ./engine
rivet test --root ./engine
rivet run --root ./engine
```

Build configures CMake and compiles the application. Test should report one passing test, `engine_test`. Run should print:

```text
Worker 0: [0, 4)
```

Save source changes before repeating these commands. From inside `engine`, you can omit `--root`; Rivet finds the project from the current directory.

## Use the current folder

To generate into an existing empty folder, use `.` as the destination:

```sh
rivet new . --standard 20 --modules false --std-modules false --preview
```

The folder name becomes the project name. Review the plan, then repeat without `--preview`. Use `--name engine` if you want another name. Rivet checks for conflicting destination files before writing.

Add `--git true` to preview and creation if you want Git initialization. Rivet reuses an enclosing repository when one exists; initialization does not stage or commit files.

See the [CLI reference](/docs/cli/) for other languages and flags, or [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) to add a component.
